+          <p>I came to policy through engineering. At <strong>Amazon</strong> I deployed machine learning routing across Spain's logistics network, and my analysis of structural disparities across ten fulfilment centres triggered a Europe-wide fairness audit. It left me with a lasting interest in the gap between what a system is said to do and what anyone can verify about it, which is the question I now bring to AI governance.</p>
+          <p>At the <strong>International Crisis Group</strong> I contributed to published analysis for the UN Security Council, the EU External Action Service and three foreign ministries, spanning Asia's militarisation, Red Sea maritime security, European security and Latin America. At the <strong>World Economic Forum</strong> I built the statistical foundation for the engagement analytics reported monthly to the Managing Board, and for <strong>UNICEF</strong> I turned Eswatini's national maternal health survey into a dashboard and map for the government.</p>
+          <p>My research now centres on AI compute and open-weight models: Europe's dependencies in the compute stack, how export controls and US–China rivalry constrain its options, and how human rights law applies to the rules for powerful models. I recently completed a Master of Public Policy at the <strong>London School of Economics</strong>, with Distinction, and presented policy recommendations on digital identity to experts at <strong>Chatham House</strong>, the Alan Turing Institute and the Tony Blair Institute.</p>
+          <p>I am French and Thai, grew up in New York, Switzerland and Hong Kong, and live in London. I work in French and English.</p>
